 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Gradient Estimation Quality:** The quality of the Phong results is heavily dependent on the gradient estimation method. Central Differences is fast but yields low-quality results; 4D Linear Regression yields higher quality but is computationally expensive [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Zero Gradient:** If the gradient magnitude is near zero, the normal is undefined. A default normal (e.g., $(0,0,1)$) must be used [G].
-*   **Ambiguous Depth Term:** The source formula includes a division by $d^\lambda$ in the final Phong equation, but $d^\lambda$ is not defined in the variable list, while $d(\mathbf{x}_i)$ is defined as the perpendicular distance. Implementers must clarify if $d^\lambda$ is a typo for $1$ or $d(\mathbf{x}_i)$ [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Gradient Estimation:** The quality of the Phong results depends heavily on the gradient estimation method. Central Differences is fast but yields low-quality normals; 4D Linear Regression yields higher quality but is computationally expensive [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Computational Cost:** The method is very expensive in time and calculation because it requires gradient precalculation (or per-sample estimation) for every single sample point and costly vector-based light intensity calculations [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Formula Ambiguity:** The source formula includes a term $d^\lambda$ in the denominator which is not defined in the variable list, only $d(x_i)$ (distance) is defined. This is a potential implementation pitfall [S1, unofficial].
 
